---
import Slide from "@/components/slides/Slide.astro";
import Code from "@/components/slides/Code.astro";
import Table from "@/components/slides/Table.astro";

const notes = "About 2 minutes. Run the query live against the frozen demo. Read the result one row at a time. The worker (812) is active, waiting on a lock (\"transactionid\" means it's waiting for another transaction to finish), and blocked_by points to 804. Find 804. Its state is \"idle in transaction\": it has a transaction open and is doing nothing. Its query column (not shown) would show the UPDATE, but that's the last statement it ran, not something it's running now. Its application name is ours. \"The database is telling us the culprit is doing nothing. Doing nothing is the bug.\" Every major database has this view; the footnote has MySQL and SQL Server. The question is always the same: who waits, and who holds?";
---

<Slide kicker="scenario 1 · the autopsy" title="step 1: ask the database who's waiting." notes={notes}>
  <Code
    lines={[
      "select pid, application_name, state, wait_event,",
      "       pg_blocking_pids(pid) as blocked_by",
      "from pg_stat_activity;",
    ]}
  />
  <Table
    code
    head={["pid", "application", "state", "wait_event", "blocked_by"]}
    rows={[
      ["812", "receipt-worker", "active", "transactionid", "{804}"],
      ["804", "orders-api", "idle in transaction", "—", "{}"],
    ]}
    highlight={1}
  />
  <p class="s-big">the lock holder is us, and it's idle. <span class="s-accent">doing nothing is the bug.</span></p>
  <p class="s-small mt-auto">illustrative output · mysql: sys.innodb_lock_waits · sql server: blocking_session_id in sys.dm_exec_requests</p>
</Slide>
